<GuidePage title='Bundle Size & Code Splitting - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 7 · Part A: Maximize the Known</div>
  <h1>Bundle Size &amp; Code Splitting</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    <a href='06-first-render-js.html'>§6</a> was about the mechanics of keeping JavaScript out of the
    first render. This chapter is about the judgment: which dependencies are worth their weight, why a
    single heavyweight component can undo the entire known/unknown split, and what all that shipped JavaScript
    actually costs the people using the app.
  </p>

  <h2 id='tax'>7.1 The JavaScript you ship is a tax on the known</h2>
  <p>
    Part A is about maximizing what the server can deliver as finished HTML. Every kilobyte of
    client JavaScript works against that goal from the other side: the browser has to download it,
    parse it, and execute it before the page is truly interactive, and until then the "known"
    content you worked so hard to render on the server is sitting there looking ready while doing
    nothing. A page can be server-rendered and <em>still</em> feel broken if it ships a megabyte of JavaScript
    in front of the first interaction. So bundle size is not a separate performance chore bolted on at
    the end; it is the same goal as the rest of Part A, measured at the network instead of the render.
  </p>
  <p>
    The good news is that the App Router splits code by route automatically: a given page ships the
    JavaScript for its own tree, not the whole app. The bad news is that this automatic splitting is
    easy to defeat, and the rest of this chapter is a tour of the four most common ways we do it:
    pulling in a component so heavy it drags the page down, fusing the known and the unknown into
    one undeferrable lump, welding an always-visible trigger to the floating panel it opens, and
    importing through barrel files that quietly negate tree-shaking.
  </p>

  <h2 id='wholesale'>7.2 The wholesale-component trap</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the most instructive failure, because it shows how bundle size and the known/unknown
    thesis are the same problem. Picture a reports page with a data table. Look at what is actually
    knowable at first render: the toolbar, the title, the column headers, the footer, the pagination
    controls, the empty-state and the row skeleton are
    <strong>all known immediately</strong>. The only genuinely unknown part is the body rows,
    because those depend on data we have to fetch.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now reach for a wholesale data grid, an all-in-one client component like MUI's
    <code>DataGrid</code> that owns the toolbar, the headers, the rows, the sorting, the virtualization,
    and the footer in a single package. You have just fused everything back together. The frame that was
    knowable is now trapped inside a client component that cannot render until its bundle downloads and
    its data arrives.
  </p>

