  <p>
    The trap is specifically that a wholesale grid takes all three of your best tools off the table
    at once. You <strong>cannot wrap Suspense around just the body rows</strong>, because the rows
    aren't a separate component. You
    <strong>cannot show a skeleton over just the rows</strong> while the header stays put, for the same
    reason. And you
    <strong>cannot defer the import of just the rows</strong> and render the known frame immediately,
    because the frame and the rows are one import. The composed version, a server-rendered table shell
    with a Suspense boundary around a server component that awaits the rows, gets all three for free.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/reports/hours/page.tsx (server component)</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { Suspense } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">HoursPage</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;table&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* The frame is known on the first byte: server HTML, no client JS. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableToolbar</span> /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableHead</span> <span class="tok-prop">columns</span>={COLUMNS} /&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* Only the rows are unknown, so only the rows suspend. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">RowSkeleton</span> <span class="tok-prop">rows</span>={10} /&gt;}&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">HoursRows</span> /&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableFooter</span> /&gt;
    &lt;/table&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

