  <h2 id='heavyweight'>7.3 Heavyweight imports, and fallbacks that stay light</h2>
  <p>
    Sometimes you genuinely need a heavy client component: a rich text editor, a charting library, a
    map, a spreadsheet grid whose interactivity you can't rebuild by hand. The rule there is not
    "never use it," it is
    <strong>keep it out of the first-render bundle and give it a light fallback</strong>. In our own <code
      >ojl-tracker</code
    > work, the data-heavy tables are loaded lazily for exactly this reason: the route paints its frame
    immediately, and the heavy grid streams in behind a skeleton rather than blocking the whole page on
    its download.
  </p>
  <p>
    The mechanics of the dynamic import (the <code>ssr: false</code> option, the top-level declaration
    rule) live in <a href='06-first-render-js.html#dynamic'>§6.3</a>; what matters here is the
    fallback.
    <strong>The fallback must be lighter than the thing it stands in for.</strong> A common mistake is
    to hand the loading state another heavy component, or a spinner that itself pulls in an animation
    library. The fallback should be cheap, static markup that reserves the right space (<a
      href='09-containers-content-shifting.html'>§9</a
    >): a plain skeleton, sized to match.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-warn'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/reports/HoursGrid.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* The heavy grid is pulled out of the first-render bundle and loaded on
   demand. Its fallback is deliberately minimal: a static skeleton that
   reserves the right height, not another heavy component. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> <span class="tok-fn">DataGrid</span> = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./HeavyDataGrid"</span>), {
  loading: () =&gt; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">RowSkeleton</span> <span class="tok-prop">rows</span>={10} /&gt;,
  ssr: <span class="tok-kw">false</span>,
});

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">HoursGrid</span>({ rows }: { rows: <span class="tok-fn">Row</span>[] }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DataGrid</span> <span class="tok-prop">rows</span>={rows} /&gt;;
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Callout kind='warn' title='Deferring a heavy import is a fallback, not a fix'>
    <p>
      Lazy-loading a wholesale grid is strictly better than shipping it in the first bundle, but it
      is still the second-best answer. It hides the weight; it doesn't remove it, and the user still
      pays the download the moment the component is needed. Whenever the frame is genuinely
      knowable, prefer the composed server-rendered table from
      <a href='#wholesale'>§7.2</a> and reserve the heavy client component for the parts that truly need
      its interactivity.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='triggers'>7.4 The trigger ships; the panel waits</h2>
  <p>
    Every floating element - a dropdown menu, a popover, a dialog, a drawer - is closed on first
    render by definition. The button that opens it is on screen from the first paint. That is the
    <a href='#tax'>§7.1</a> question answered twice, in opposite directions, by two things that sit
    an inch apart in the same mock. The structural consequence:
    <strong
      >the trigger and the panel it opens should be separate components, side by side,</strong
    > so the trigger can ship in the first-paint bundle while the panel's import is deferred until someone
    actually clicks. <a href='15-component-breakdown.html#worked'>§15.2</a>'s card menu is exactly this
    shape: a trigger file that owns the open state, and a panel file behind a dynamic import.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shape that quietly fights this is the compound-component API most UI libraries document,
    because it nests the trigger <em>inside</em> the floating component. Tracker's action menus
    follow it faithfully:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-bad'
      is:raw><span class="filename">OrgActionsMenu.tsx - the panel rides in the button's bundle</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> {
  Button,
  DropdownMenu,
  DropdownMenuContent,
  DropdownMenuItem,
  DropdownMenuTrigger,
} <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@tracker/ui"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">OrgActionsMenu</span>({ org }: { org: Organization }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenu</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuTrigger</span> <span class="tok-prop">asChild</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span> <span class="tok-prop">size</span>=<span class="tok-str">"sm"</span>&gt;Organization actions&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuTrigger</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuContent</span> <span class="tok-prop">align</span>=<span class="tok-str">"end"</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">onSelect</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">startEdit</span>(org.id)}&gt;Edit&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">onSelect</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">deactivate</span>(org.id)}&gt;Deactivate&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span>&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuContent</span>&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenu</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      To be fair to the library: it already defers the <em>render</em>. <code
        >DropdownMenuContent</code
      > doesn't enter the DOM until the menu opens. What it cannot defer is the <em>download</em>.
      Because the panel lives in the same file as the always-visible button, every item, icon, and
      handler is statically reachable from the first paint, and ships with it.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    For a two-item text menu the freight is small (though it multiplies by every row of a table that
    renders one of these). The real damage is that the welded shape gets reused as the panel grows.
    Tracker's email-change flow is a button that opens a sheet, and because the sheet nests inside
    the trigger's file, that one button statically imports the form library, the schema-validation
    library, its resolver, and the entire sheet component - none of which can do anything until a
    click that most visits never make. The fix is not to abandon the library's API; it is to cut the
    file at the same seam the user experiences:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">OrgActionsMenu.tsx - the trigger owns the state; the panel is a deferred sibling</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { Button, DropdownMenu, DropdownMenuTrigger } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@tracker/ui"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">const</span> OrgActionsMenuPanel = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt;
  <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./OrgActionsMenuPanel"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.OrgActionsMenuPanel)
);

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">OrgActionsMenu</span>({ org }: { org: Organization }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [open, setOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenu</span> <span class="tok-prop">open</span>={open} <span class="tok-prop">onOpenChange</span>={setOpen}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuTrigger</span> <span class="tok-prop">asChild</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span> <span class="tok-prop">size</span>=<span class="tok-str">"sm"</span>&gt;Organization actions&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuTrigger</span>&gt;
      {open &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">OrgActionsMenuPanel</span> <span class="tok-prop">org</span>={org} /&gt;}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenu</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The root and trigger stay inline: they are cheap and genuinely needed at first paint. The
      panel becomes a controlled, conditionally rendered sibling, so <code>dynamic</code> is never
      even asked for its module until the first open.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">OrgActionsMenuPanel.tsx - everything the closed menu doesn't need</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { DropdownMenuContent, DropdownMenuItem } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@tracker/ui"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">OrgActionsMenuPanel</span>({ org }: { org: Organization }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuContent</span> <span class="tok-prop">align</span>=<span class="tok-str">"end"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">onSelect</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">startEdit</span>(org.id)}&gt;Edit&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span> <span class="tok-prop">onSelect</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">deactivate</span>(org.id)}&gt;Deactivate&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuItem</span>&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">DropdownMenuContent</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The compound API still works: the panel renders inside the same <code>DropdownMenu</code> root,
      so the library's context reaches it. Only the import graph changed - and for the sheet version
      of this pattern, this file is where the form library, the schema, and the sheet body live,
      which is precisely the weight the button no longer carries.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Two judgment notes. First, the first open now pays the chunk's download; for panels this is
    almost always invisible (they are small relative to what they saved, and the fetch races the
    user's hand), but it is the same trade as <a href='#heavyweight'>§7.3</a>: don't split a
    three-line panel out of reflex, split when the panel has weight. Second, when you design your
    <em>own</em> trigger-plus-floating components, bake the seam into the API from the start: a
    trigger that owns the open state and mounts a lazy panel, as in
    <a href='15-component-breakdown.html#worked'>§15.2</a>, rather than a component that needs the
    panel's module just to render the button. It is <a href='#wholesale'>§7.2</a>'s lesson rotated
    ninety degrees: don't weld the always-visible to the almost-never-visible.
  </p>

  <h2 id='barrels'>7.5 Barrel files quietly defeat code splitting</h2>
  <p>
    A barrel file is an <code>index.ts</code> that re-exports everything in a folder so callers can write
    <code>import &#123; Button &#125; from "@/ui"</code> instead of reaching into the specific module.
    It reads nicely and it is a reliable way to inflate every bundle that touches it. The problem is that
    importing one name from a barrel makes your route depend on the <em>whole</em> barrel: the bundler
    now has to consider every module the barrel re-exports, and anything with a side effect, or anything
    tree-shaking can't prove is unused, comes along for the ride.
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Barrel: one name pulls the whole package'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">components/Toolbar.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { Button } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/ui"</span>;</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          Pulling from the @/ui barrel adds the whole library to this route's graph: Button drags in
          Modal, DatePicker, RichTextEditor, and everything else the barrel re-exports.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ Direct: one name pulls one module'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">components/Toolbar.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { Button } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"@/ui/button"</span>;</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          Imports only Button's module; nothing else in @/ui joins this route's bundle.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    Modern bundlers and Next's <code>optimizePackageImports</code> can claw some of this back, and for
    well-behaved side-effect-free packages the damage is smaller than it used to be. But the reliable
    habit is to not create the problem: import from the specific module, and
    <strong>don't ship <code>index.ts</code> barrels for your own component libraries</strong>. This
    is also where bundle size meets component design: barrels tend to grow around files that hold
    too many components, which is the same instinct
    <a href='15-component-breakdown.html'>§15</a>
    argues against. One component per file, imported directly, is both cleaner to read and cheaper to
    ship.
  </p>

  <h2 id='cost'>7.6 What bloated pages actually cost</h2>
  <p>
    It is worth being concrete about who pays, because "big bundle" sounds abstract and the costs
    are not. They land on the two groups we care most about: the people using the app and the people
    building it.
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style='width: 26%'>Who pays</th>
        <th>What it costs them</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Users, on first load</strong></td>
        <td>
          A longer wait before the page is interactive, even after the server HTML is on screen. The
          gap between "looks ready" and "responds to a click" is pure JavaScript download, parse,
          and execution time.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Users, on every navigation</strong></td>
        <td>
          Heavier route bundles make client transitions feel sluggish, and the pain scales with the
          worst device and network in your user base, not your laptop on office wifi. Mobile and
          poor connections feel it first and worst.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Users, on data</strong></td>
        <td>
          Megabytes of JavaScript are megabytes of someone's mobile data plan and battery, spent
          re-shipping code that a server render would have turned into a few kilobytes of HTML.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>The team, on build</strong></td>
        <td>
          Bigger dependency graphs mean slower builds and slower type-checks, a tax on every commit
          and every CI run, and a compounding drag on how fast the team can move.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Keep the knowable frame out of client components.</strong> Render toolbars, headers,
          and footers as server HTML; wrap only the unknown parts in a boundary. Don't reach for a wholesale
          grid that fuses them.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Heavy client components load on demand,</strong> out of the first-render bundle, behind
          a fallback that is lighter than what it replaces.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Triggers and their floating panels are separate files.</strong> The button ships at
          first paint; the menu, dialog, or sheet it opens is a deferred sibling behind a dynamic import,
          not a nested child of the trigger's module.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Import from the specific module, not a barrel.</strong> Don't ship
          <code>index.ts</code> barrels for your own component libraries; they defeat tree-shaking.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Measure first-render JS as a budget,</strong> not an afterthought; a server-rendered
          page that ships a megabyte of JavaScript is not a fast page.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='imp' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

